\section*{I. INTRODUCTION}

Machine learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs) have introduced a new paradigm for all-atom molecular dynamics simulations, offering the opportunity to avoid expensive quantum mechanical calculations while preserving accuracy levels as in \(a b\) initio molecular dynamics. \({ }^{1,2}\) Accurate modeling of long-range (LR) interactions remains a fundamental challenge in MLIPs, as most existing models are built on local atomic environments and do not adequately account for LR interactions that decay slowly with distance, \({ }^{3,4}\) including Coulomb, dispersion, and dipole-dipole interactions. LR interactions play an essential role in determining the structure, dynamics, and thermodynamic properties of complex systems such as electrochemical interfaces, electronically driven phase transitions, protein folding, and polar materials. \({ }^{5-9}\)

Various approaches have been developed in the literature in order to incorporate LR effects into MLIPs. Improving local models
with empirical corrections for electrostatics and dispersion \({ }^{10,11}\) allows us to take into account these LR effects, but this idea remains a difficulty for its transferability to different environments. The fourth-generation high-dimensional neural network potentials (4G-HDNNPs) \({ }^{6}\) propose to predict effective atomic partial charges while enforcing global charge conservation by using the charge equilibration scheme. \({ }^{12}\) In the Deep Potential (DP) LR model \({ }^{13}\) and selfconsistent field neural networks, \({ }^{14}\) it was suggested to learn virtual charges at positions of maximally localized Wannier centers. Once these charges are learned, electrostatic interactions are computed using the fast Fourier transform (FFT)-based Ewald summation, \({ }^{15,16}\) which enables fast LR force evaluation. The long-distance equivariant method \({ }^{7,17}\) is an alternative approach that does not rely on explicit charge assignment but incorporates LR effects via local representations and an LR message-passing model. \({ }^{18}\) This is achieved by coupling with learnable frequency filters in reciprocal space. By the
"nearsightedness principle" \({ }^{19}\) rooted in electronic structure theory, the latent Ewald summation (LES) method \({ }^{20}\) also provides an elegant approach that learns the so-called "latent variable" \(\boldsymbol{q}\) from local atomic features. Different from previous works that explicitly learn charges, \(\boldsymbol{q}\) in the LES can be high dimensional to represent parameters for more generalized LR interactions. These \(\boldsymbol{q}\) then interact also via the Ewald summation to determine the global energy.

Despite these advances, there are many open problems for learning LR interactions. \({ }^{21}\) Several studies have shown that LR interactions in realistic systems often deviate from idealized power-law decay. \({ }^{4,7}\) This discrepancy arises in part because atomic charges in density functional theory (DFT) reflect spatially distributed electron densities, rather than point-like sources. The widely used Ewald summation, originally designed for \(1 / r\) Coulombic interactions, is not well suited to capture generalized LR decay rates. Recently, we proposed the sum-of-Gaussians neural network (SOG-Net) \({ }^{22}\) to learn LR interactions with different decay tails. Similar to the LES, the SOG-Net predicts a latent variable \(\boldsymbol{q}\) from local atomic features using an embedded neural network, \({ }^{23}\) without relying on predefined atomic charges. Differently, the SOG-Net introduces a reciprocalspace convolution layer where the Fourier multiplier is expressed as a sum of Gaussians with trainable amplitudes and bandwidths. This design allows capturing a broad range of LR interaction profiles while retaining near-linear computational scaling through the use of non-uniform fast Fourier transforms (NUFFTs). \({ }^{24,25}\) Although the SOG-Net is, in principle, compatible with various short-range (SR) MLIP architectures, \({ }^{26-29}\) it has so far been implemented with the DP descriptor. \({ }^{23}\) Performance evaluations using alternative SR descriptors, such as graph neural networks (GNNs) and body-order expansion-based descriptors, remain unexplored.

In this paper, we extend the SOG-Net framework by integrating it with the Cartesian atomic cluster expansion (CACE) descriptor, \({ }^{30}\) resulting in a new CACE-SOG model. The CACE is a messagepassing, graph-based, explicit SR descriptor that is invariant to rotation, translation, and permutation. Compared with the fully data-driven DP descriptor, explicit descriptors such as ACE and CACE often require fewer trainable parameters and provide higher data efficiency. \({ }^{31,32}\) Relative to ACE, CACE offers a mathematically well-defined and more efficient route for MLIP design by working in Cartesian coordinates while preserving rotational symmetry and body orders; it is also straightforward to implement and to extend with new features. Because SOG-Net uses the SR descriptor to learn latent variables, pairing it with explicit CACE descriptors can improve both training and simulation efficiency while maintaining accuracy.

To our knowledge, current LR models built on such explicit SR descriptors rely on deterministic Ewald summation; \({ }^{33}\) we are not aware of a model that couples them with a data-driven LR module such as SOG-Net. Recently, the CACE-based latent Ewald summation (CACE-LES) \({ }^{20}\) has been shown to capture electrostatics and to recover effective charges and polarization effects from energy/force data. \({ }^{34,35}\) Replacing Ewald summation with SOG-Net allows more general LR effects within a unified framework while keeping data efficiency. We validate the performance of the CACE-SOG, demonstrating that the model inherits the data efficiency and physical consistency of graph-based neural networks while introducing a flexible and learnable mechanism to capture non-Coulombic LR interactions. Importantly, for systems with Coulomb interactions,
the CACE-SOG achieves similar accuracy as the CACE-LES. When the system includes non-Coulombic LR interactions, the CACE-SOG shows strong superiority. The accuracy of the new model is similar to the DP-based SOG-Net \({ }^{22}\) but with fewer trainable parameters, further demonstrating that the SOG-Net is a general framework for LR learning. These findings suggest that the SOG-Net is promising for modeling LR interactions by incorporating existing SR descriptors in a uniform framework.

\section*{II. METHODS}

Consider an atomic system of \(N\) atoms located at \(\boldsymbol{r}_{1}, \cdots, \boldsymbol{r}_{N}\) in the domain \(\Omega\) with periodic boundary conditions. The total potential energy is decomposed into SR and LR components: \(E=E^{\mathrm{sr}}+E^{\mathrm{lr}}\). The SR energy \(E^{\mathrm{sr}}\) is expressed as a sum over atomic contributions,
\[
E^{\mathrm{sr}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E_{\mathrm{SR}, i}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{SR}}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\right)
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) is the local atomic environment descriptor for atom \(i\), and \(f_{\theta_{\mathrm{SR}}}(\cdot)\) is a fully connected neural network parameterized by \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{SR}}\) that maps the descriptor to the atomic energy. The LR energy \(E^{\mathrm{lr}}\) is often modeled in Fourier space. In this work, we introduce the CACE and the SOG-Net to treat SR and LR components, respectively, by making an explicit connection between the two networks.

\section*{A. Short-range descriptor}

The SR descriptors have been broadly studied in Refs. 23, 26-29, and 36-38, and here we adopt a newly developed CACE by Cheng \({ }^{30}\) as the SR descriptor for constructing the machine-learning potential of LR systems. Different from the \(\mathrm{ACE}^{29}\) and most of the other E(3) equivariant MPNNs that rely on spherical harmonics as an angular basis, the CACE encodes angular dependence in a Cartesian basis, avoiding the use of spherical transformations, tensor products, and Clebsch-Gordan matrices by the Cartesian atomic cluster expansion. It provides a simple and efficient way to describe many-body interactions such that it is invariant to rotation, translation, and permutation. These advantages motivate our choice of the CACE as the SR descriptor, in spite of the fact that the network structure presented in this work can, in principle, be combined with any of the existing SR descriptors.

In the CACE descriptor, each local atomic environment is represented as a graph, with atoms as nodes. For neighbors within a cutoff radius \(r_{\text {cut }}\), directed edges connect each neighbor \(j\) to the central atom \(i\). Each atomic state is defined as \(\sigma_{i}=\left(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_{i}, \boldsymbol{h}_{i}\right)\), with \(\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\) being the Cartesian position, \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{i}\) being an embedding of the chemical element, and \(\boldsymbol{h}_{i}\) being the learnable features. To construct \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{i}\), the element \(\alpha_{i}\) is first encoded as a one-hot vector of length \(N_{\text {element }}\), which is then mapped by a learnable weight matrix of size \(N_{\text {embedding }} \times N_{\text {element }}\) to obtain the continuous embedding of length \(N_{\text {embedding }}\). The atom-centered basis is obtained by summing over all incoming edges,
\[
A_{i, c n \mu}=\sum_{j \in \mathscr{N}_{(i)}} \chi_{c n \mu}\left(\sigma_{i}, \sigma_{j}\right)
\]
where \(\mathscr{N}(i)\) is the neighbor set of \(i\). The edge basis \(\chi_{\text {cn } \mu}\) is indexed by \(c\) (edge channel), \(n\) (radial basis), and \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\) (angular momentum).

It encodes the spatial arrangement of atom \(j\) around atom \(i\) as the product of an edge-type, a radial, and an angular bases,
\[
\chi_{c n \mu}\left(\sigma_{i}, \sigma_{j}\right)=T_{c}\left(\sigma_{i}, \sigma_{j}\right) R_{n, c \mu}\left(r_{j i}\right) \mathcal{L}_{\mu}\left(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{r}}_{j i}\right)
\]
where \(\tilde{\boldsymbol{r}}_{j i}=\boldsymbol{r}_{j i} / r_{j i}\) with \(\boldsymbol{r}_{j i}=\boldsymbol{r}_{j}-\boldsymbol{r}_{i}\) and \(r_{j i}=\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{j i}\right|\). The edge-type basis \(T_{c}\) depends on the chemical elements and, if message-passing layers are used, also on the hidden features, with \(c\) labeling the edge feature dimension. The radial basis \(R_{n, c \mu}\) is a set of \(n\) trainable Bessel functions multiplied by a smooth cutoff function. Let \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{r}}_{j i}=\left(x_{j i}, y_{j i}, z_{j i}\right)\), and \(\boldsymbol{\mu}=\left(\mu_{x}, \mu_{y}, \mu_{z}\right)\). The angular basis is defined as
\[
\mathscr{L}_{\boldsymbol{\mu}}\left(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{r}}_{j i}\right)=x_{j i}^{\mu_{x}} y_{j i}^{\mu_{y}} z_{j i}^{\mu_{z}}
\]
with \(\mu_{x}+\mu_{y}+\mu_{z}=\mu\) being the total number of angular momenta. Once the atom-centered basis is constructed, it is symmetrized to produce invariant features \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) with a given body order \(v\) such that \(v\)-body correlations are involved in the features. The series is truncated at \(\mu_{\text {max }}\) and \(v_{\text {max }}\), depending on the requirement of accuracy and efficiency.

By construction, \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) forms a complete basis of rotation-, translation-, and permutation-invariant functions of the local environment, which is input into a fully connected network for training, such that the network can describe the SR interactions of atomic systems.

\section*{B. Long-range descriptor}

To incorporate LR interactions, we use another neural network \(f_{\theta_{\mathrm{q}}}(\cdot)\) that maps the same local descriptor \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) to a latent variable \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}=\left(q_{i, 1}, \ldots, q_{i, P}\right)\) of dimension \(P\) for each atom,
\[
\boldsymbol{q}_{i}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{q}}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\right)
\]

The underlying physical principle is the nearsightedness principle rooted in electronic structure theory, \({ }^{19}\) which states that local electronic properties, such as the electron density, are strongly influenced only by the effective potential in the nearby environment. Although the principle is empirical, it has been shown to be effective in many applications. \({ }^{3}\) The latent variable \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) is multi-dimensional and is designed to encode features relevant to LR interactions. When \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) is restricted to a one-dimensional scalar, previous work has shown that it can be interpreted as an effective atomic charge when used as input to the Ewald summation for computing electrostatic interactions. \({ }^{34}\)

Now we consider how the latent variable \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) is utilized to compute LR energy contributions. Suppose that \(L_{x}, L_{y}\), and \(L_{z}\) are the system sizes along the three directions. In our SOG-Net, by leveraging the rapid decay of LR interactions in Fourier space, we account for the LR energy by using multiple convolution layers,
\[
E^{\mathrm{lr}}=\frac{1}{2 V} \sum_{\eta=1}^{P} \sum_{\boldsymbol{k}} \widehat{f}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{SOG}}}(\boldsymbol{k})|\boldsymbol{S}(\boldsymbol{k}, \eta)|^{2}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{k}=2 \pi\left(m_{x} / L_{x}, m_{y} / L_{y}, m_{z} / L_{z}\right)\) denotes the Fourier modes and runs over all integers \(m_{x}, m_{y}\), and \(m_{z}\), and the structure factor \(\boldsymbol{S}(\boldsymbol{k}, \eta)\) is defined as
\[
\boldsymbol{S}(\boldsymbol{k}, \eta)=\sum_{i=1}^{N} q_{i, \eta} e^{-\imath \boldsymbol{k} \cdot \boldsymbol{r}_{i}}
\]
with \(\iota\) being the imaginary unit. \(\widehat{f}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {SOG }}}(\boldsymbol{k})\) is the Fourier multiplier parameterized as a sum of Gaussians,
\[
\widehat{f}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{SOG}}}(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{\ell=0}^{M-1} w_{\ell} e^{-k^{2} / s_{\ell}^{2}}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {SOG }}=\left\{w_{\ell}, s_{\ell}\right\}_{\ell=0}^{M-1}\) is the set of trainable parameters. If \(P>1\) for the latent variable, \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {SOG }}\) can also be multilayer, which serves as an approximation of the \(L R\) tail in Fourier space. The form of the \(L R\) energy, as constructed above, is also inherently invariant to rotation, translation, and permutation.

Figure 1 illustrates a sketch map of the network architecture of the CACE-SOG model, where the CACE descriptor is used for both the SR energy and the latent variable, and the SOG-Net is used to generate the Fourier multiplier. The architecture is similar to the network in Ji et al., \({ }^{22}\) but with the CACE as the SR descriptor. To accelerate the evaluation, a fast algorithm for SOG-Net can be embedded in the training procedure and molecular simulations, which maps discrete latent variables onto a uniform grid, applies an FFT, performs diagonal scaling, and then applies an IFFT. The resulting field is subsequently remapped to the positions of the variables. This algorithm follows our previous work \({ }^{22}\) and can be interpreted as a type-1 NUFFT followed by a type-2 NUFFT. \({ }^{25}\) The FINUFFT package \({ }^{39}\) is used in practical implementation.

The CACE-SOG model allows for physically meaningful interpretations and the extraction of quantities such as partial charges, dipole moments, and quadrupole moments under specific conditions. In particular, when the latent variable \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) is restricted to a single layer, its learned values can be interpreted physically. For instance, \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) may represent atomic partial charges or dispersion coefficients if the LR component of the potential energy surface is dominated by classical electrostatics ( \(1 / r\) ) or dispersion interactions ( \(1 / r^{6}\) ). In this context, the corresponding SOG multiplier \(\widehat{f}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {SOG }}}\) serves as an approximation of the LR interaction kernel in Fourier space. In quantum-mechanical systems, such as those based on DFT, the combination of the latent variable and the Fourier multiplier in the CACE-SOG model can be viewed as a coarse-grained representation of electron density polarization. In both classical and quantum settings, if the latent variable is restricted to a single layer, it can be used to compute dipole and quadrupole moments. With multiple latent layers, the model captures more complex LR decays, but extracting charges and multipoles becomes difficult. It reflects a trade-off between interpretability and accuracy: although explicit multipole extraction becomes less feasible, the model gains flexibility to achieve higher accuracy in energy and force predictions. This design allows the SOG-Net to learn a wide range of interaction patterns without explicitly fitting predefined physical laws or multipole expansions.

Charge conservation is an important issue in simulations. In the Ewald-type method, the net charge can be treated as a uniform background with the zero-frequency term being removed to ensure the well-posedness of the energy. Such removal is not necessary in the CACE-SOG model, as the SOG multiplier is smooth in both real and Fourier spaces. From another perspective, the latent variable \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) in the CACE-SOG is not limited to representing partial charges and may follow more general rules. Therefore, the constraint \(\sum q_{i, \eta}=0\) is not strictly required. In all our tests, we did not observe loss of accuracy or artifacts from omitting this constraint.

