In contrast, for the MLIP models that explicitly learn charges, such as 3G-HDNNP, the lack of charge equilibration may result in dramatically larger errors. \({ }^{6}\) If needed, neutrality can be enforced for CACE-SOG by adding a penalty term to the total potential energy, \(E_{\mathrm{C}}=\lambda\left(Q-\sum q_{i, \eta}\right)^{2}\), where \(\lambda>0\) is a Lagrangian multiplier and \(Q\) is the referenced quantity of the system, enforcing global conservation of the desired quantity.

Another challenge is handling different charge states. In CACESOG and most MLIPs, the local descriptor \(\left\{\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\right\}\) does not encode the net charge or oxidation state. It can cause problems when handling systems with varying charge states simultaneously. \({ }^{6,34}\) To resolve this, we adopt the strategy of King et al.: \({ }^{34}\) when the training set contains multiple net charges, we concatenate the total net charge \(Q\) to the local descriptor \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i}\) and use \(\boldsymbol{D}_{i} \oplus Q\) as input to predict the short-range energy and latent variable,
\[
E^{\mathrm{sr}}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{SR}}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i} \oplus Q\right) \text { and } \boldsymbol{q}_{i}=f_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{q}}}\left(\boldsymbol{D}_{i} \oplus Q\right),
\]
without modifying the SOG-Net architecture. The predicted \(\boldsymbol{q}_{i}\) are then substituted into Eq. (6) to compute the long-range energy. The above strategy makes the model aware of the system charge and can represent potential energy surfaces associated with different charge states.

\section*{C. Initialization in training procedure}

In our previous work, \({ }^{22}\) a simple initialization strategy was used for \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {SOG. }}\). In particular, the weights \(\left\{w_{\ell}\right\}\) were set to unity, and the bandwidths \(\left\{s_{\ell}\right\}\) were initialized as a logarithmically spaced sequence. This heuristic approach can lead to significant deviations from the true decay behavior. Here, we propose a more effective initialization scheme based on prior knowledge of the expected LR
decay. In particular, we assume a plausible power-law form close to the target interaction and use the parameters derived from an SOG approximation to the corresponding kernel as the initial guess. By incorporating physical insight into the initialization, this method improves the representation of LR interactions and substantially reduces the training time.

We adopt the bilateral series approximation (BSA), \({ }^{40}\) which starts from an integral representation of a power-law kernel,
\[
\frac{1}{r^{2 \alpha}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-e^{\tau} r^{2}+\alpha \tau} d \tau
\]
where \(\Gamma(\cdot)\) denotes the Gamma function. In particular, the case \(\alpha=1 / 2\) corresponds to the Coulombic kernel \(1 / r\). Applying the trapezoidal rule to Eq. (10) with quadrature points \(x_{m}=t_{0}+m \zeta\), where \(t_{0}=-\log \left(2 s^{2}\right)\) and \(\zeta=2 \log b\) for \(s>0\) and \(b>1\), yields the BSA of the \(1 / r\) kernel,
\[
\frac{1}{r} \approx \mathscr{F}_{\mathrm{SOG}}(r)=\frac{2 \log b}{\sqrt{2 \pi s^{2}}} \sum_{m=-\infty}^{\infty} b^{-m} e^{-\frac{r^{2}}{2 b^{2 m} s^{2}}},
\]
where terms with \(m<0\) capture the SR contributions, while terms with \(m \geq 0\) correspond to the LR components. One important feature of Eq. (11) is that the pointwise relative error has the asymptotic bound as \(b \rightarrow 1\) for all \(r>0,{ }^{40}\)
\[
\mathscr{E}_{r}:=\frac{\left|1 / r-\mathscr{F}_{\mathrm{SOG}}(r)\right|}{|1 / r|} \leq 2 \sqrt{2} e^{-\frac{\pi^{2}}{2 \log b}} .
\]

Once the infinite sum in the right-hand side of Eq. (11) is truncated at \(0 \leq m \leq M-1\), the resulting SOG, \(\mathscr{F}_{\mathrm{SOG}}^{M}\), is an approximation of the LR component of \(1 / r\) in real space. In our MLIP training, we set \(b=2\) and \(s=r_{\text {cut }} / 1.9892536839080267\), so that \(\mathscr{E}_{r} \leq 0.001\) for
\(r>r_{\text {cut }}\). The double-precision constant is chosen so that \(\mathscr{F}_{\mathrm{SOG}}^{M}\left(r_{\text {cut }}\right)=1 / r_{\text {cut }}\) holds exactly when \(b=2\); this enforces continuity at the cutoff and reduces the approximation error. \({ }^{41,42}\) We then compute the Fourier transform of \(\mathscr{F}_{\mathrm{SOG}}^{M}\) in closed form,
\[
\hat{\mathcal{F}}_{\mathrm{SOG}}^{M}(\boldsymbol{k})=\sum_{m=0}^{M-1} 4 \pi b^{2 m} s^{2} \log b e^{-b^{2 m} s^{2} k^{2} / 2}
\]
which is again a sum of Gaussians. We use this to initialize the Fourier multiplier \(\widehat{f}_{\text {SOG }}\) in Eq. (8) by setting the initial weights and bandwidths to
\[
w_{\ell}=4 \pi b^{2 \ell} s^{2} \log b, \quad s_{\ell}=\sqrt{\frac{2}{b^{2 \ell} s^{2}}}, \quad \ell=0, \ldots, M-1
\]
i.e., \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text {SOG }}=\left\{w_{\ell}, s_{\ell}\right\}_{\ell=0}^{M-1}\). Detailed derivation of Eqs. (10)-(13) is provided in Sec. I of the supplementary material.

At the start of training, we choose \(M\) large enough to keep the truncation error of the Coulomb-energy tail below a target tolerance \(\varepsilon\),
\[
\left|\sum_{\boldsymbol{n} \in \mathbb{Z}^{3}} \sum_{\substack{i, j=1 \\\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{j i}^{n}\right|>r_{\text {cut }}}}^{N} q_{i} q_{j}\left(\frac{1}{\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{\boldsymbol{j} i}{ }^{\boldsymbol{n}}\right|}-\mathscr{F}_{\mathrm{SOG}}^{M}\left(\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{j i}^{\boldsymbol{n}}\right|\right)\right)\right| \leq \varepsilon\left|\sum_{\boldsymbol{n} \in \mathbb{Z}^{3}} \sum_{i, j=1}^{N} \frac{q_{i} q_{j}}{\left|\boldsymbol{r}_{\boldsymbol{j i}}^{\boldsymbol{n}}\right|}\right|,
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{r}_{j i}^{\boldsymbol{n}}:=\boldsymbol{r}_{\boldsymbol{j} \boldsymbol{i}}+\boldsymbol{n} L\) and the \(\boldsymbol{n}\)-loops cover all periodic images. By default, we take \(M=12\), which in our tests keeps the LR-tail energy error at \(\varepsilon=10^{-3}\). Higher accuracy is achievable by choosing \(b\) closer to 1 , but this typically requires finer Fourier grids and a larger \(M\), \({ }^{42}\) leading to higher computational cost. This physics-guided initialization acts as a simple "normalization" prior on the LR multipliers, making the initial SOG multiplier closer to the true LR decay and, thus, speeding up training and helping prevent overfitting.

If a different inverse power-law kernel besides \(1 / r\) is desired for the initial guess of \(f_{\theta_{\text {SOG }}}\), one can discretize the integral in Eq. (10) with the corresponding \(\alpha\) in the same manner. For simplicity, we initialize the SOG parameters using the BSA for the \(1 / r\) kernel in all tests reported in this work, and we find this sufficient. More generally, we suggest \(1 / r\)-based BSA initialization as a default setup, since electrostatics are common in LR systems and the \(1 / r\) BSA provides a physically reasonable LR decay compared with naïve or random initialization.

After training, we apply model reduction \({ }^{43}\) to find the optimal number of Gaussians required to approximate the trained SOG multiplier within a prescribed tolerance. In practice, \(M\) can often be cut by about half without loss of accuracy. We have included a brief description of the model reduction procedure in Sec. II of the supplementary material. For details on the reduction of the SOG series, see Refs. 43-46. A Python implementation of the model reduction procedure is included in our SOG-Net package (https://github.com/DuktigYajie/SOG-Net/tree/main/CACE-SOG/model_reduction).

Note that the BSA approximation has been employed in accelerating both classical and quantum many-body LR calculations. \({ }^{41,42,47-49}\) For kernels without an explicit form, as shown in Eq. (10), kernel-independent SOG methods \({ }^{45,50}\) can be employed to construct an appropriate approximation. As shown in our
numerical experiments, this physics-informed initialization may significantly improve convergence speed during the model training.

\section*{III. RESULTS}

\section*{A. Molecular dimers}

We first evaluate the CACE-SOG model on a dataset of molecular dimers, \({ }^{51}\) which is widely used to benchmark MLIPs in capturing LR interactions across diverse chemical environments. \({ }^{7,20}\) The dataset includes energy and force information computed using the HSE06 hybrid DFT functional with a many-body dispersion correction. The original dataset consists of six types of dimers, each exhibiting distinct ideal power-law decay exponents of the form \(1 / r^{p}\). Here, we focus on three representative types: charge-charge (CC, \(p=1\) ), polar-polar (PP, \(p=3\) ), and apolar-apolar (AA, \(p=6\) ). The training set includes ten configurations with intermolecular separations ranging from approximately \(5 \AA\) to \(12 \AA\), while the test set consists of three configurations with separations between \(12 \AA\) and 15 Å.

We analyze the binding energy curves and atomic forces for the CACE-only, the CACE-LES, and the CACE-SOG models. The CACE representation uses a cutoff \(r_{\text {cut }}=5 \AA\), 6 Bessel radial functions, \(c=8, \mu_{\max }=2, v_{\max }=2, N_{\text {embedding }}=3\), and one messagepassing layer ( \(T=1\) ). Since the two molecules can have a distance beyond the cutoff of \(r_{\text {cut }}=5 \AA\), message passing does not help for this task. We keep these settings to match prior CACE-LES work, \({ }^{20,34}\) so the SR part is identical across models and the comparison between CACE-LES and CACE-SOG is fair. For details of these parameters, see Ref. 30. For CACE-LES, we use a one-dimensional ( \(P=1\) ) latent variable and an Ewald smearing factor \(\sigma=1 \AA\). The original CACELES paper \({ }^{20}\) uses a four-dimensional latent variable ( \(P=4\) ), while its model test error is comparable with \(P=1 .{ }^{34}\) For the CACE-SOG, we use \(M=12\) Gaussians and use the same grid as in CACE-LES. Similar to CACE-LES, we also use a one-dimensional \((P=1)\) latent variable \(q\), which is sufficient for our CACE-SOG model. Figure 2 shows the binding energy prediction errors for each configuration, with the mean absolute errors (MAEs) for energy and forces over all configurations summarized in Table I. MLIPs with LR descriptors are much more accurate than the CACE-only model. The Ewaldbased CACE-LES model performs exceptionally well on the CC dimer class, slightly outperforming the CACE-SOG on the test configurations. For dimer types such as PP and AA, whose interactions deviate from the \(1 / r\) form, the CACE-SOG demonstrates a clear advantage. Its kernel-based representation more effectively captures the LR tails of interactions governed by higher-order power-law decay (e.g., \(1 / r^{3}\) and \(1 / r^{6}\) ), highlighting its superior adaptability to diverse physical regimes. The learned deviation of SOG-Net from the BSA initialization reflects both finite- \(M\) truncation effects and a data-driven effective correction for deviations of the true LR tail from the ideal decay.

Relative to the DP-SOG, \({ }^{22}\) the CACE-SOG replaces the fully data-driven DP SR descriptor with the explicit CACE descriptor. This reduces trainable parameters and training-time memory, since backpropagation no longer stores large neighbor-pair activation stacks. Empirically, the CACE-SOG reaches the same or better accuracy with fewer parameters. We report trainable-parameter counts for the tested MLIPs in the first three rows of Table II; the DP-SR and DP-SOG numbers are taken from our previous work. \({ }^{22}\) On the

