\section*{B. Potassium fluoride aqueous solutions}

To further assess the accuracy and generalizability of the CACE-SOG, we use a dataset comprising aqueous potassium fluoride (KF) systems \({ }^{34}\) spanning a wide concentration range from 0 to \(\sim 2 \mathrm{~mol} / \mathrm{L}\). The dataset includes configurations of both bulk electrolytes and interfacial electrolyte-vapor systems. The reference energies and forces of all systems were calculated using the flexible SPC/Fw water model with fixed charges of -0.8476 e on oxygen atoms and +0.4238 e on hydrogen atoms, together with monovalent potassium and fluoride ions. Figure 3(a) presents the two snapshots in the dataset. The presence of an interface and multiple species introduces nontrivial screening effects, making it a suitable dataset to test our model.

We calculated the results with the CACE-only, CACE-LES, and CACE-SOG, using the same CACE representation for all three MLIPs: 6 Bessel radial functions with \(c=12, \mu_{\text {max }}=3, v_{\text {max }}=3\), \(N_{\text {embedding }}=4\), no message-passing ( \(T=0\) ) or one message-passing layer ( \(T=1\) ), and a cutoff of \(r_{\text {cut }}=4.5 \AA\). The setup of the LR component in both the CACE-LES and CACE-SOG follows the same setup as in the molecular dimer tests. Figure 3(b) shows that the CACE-SOG model with a cutoff radius of \(r_{\text {cut }}=4.5 \AA\) accurately predicts atomic charges as the training set size increases. Figure 3(c) demonstrates that the predicted atomic forces match the reference values closely, achieving a coefficient of determination of \(R^{2}=0.999994\). This indicates that the model effectively captures the force landscape across all atomic species. Figures 3(d) and 3(e) compare the performance of the CACE-SOG with the CACE-LES, where the CACE-LES results were obtained from publicly available
data on GitHub. \({ }^{34}\) Figure 3(d) illustrates that the CACE-SOG significantly improves the MAE over an order of magnitude in charge predictions in most cases with a varying number of samples. This is reasonable because screening in interfacial water makes the LR decay depart from the ideal \(1 / r\). The CACE-LES uses the classical Ewald summation for a fixed \(1 / r\) kernel suited to bulk and is less flexible for such tails, while the CACE-SOG learns a data-driven multiplier that adapts to non-Coulombic decay, yielding more accurate inferred charges. The CACE-only model has no latent-variable module, so it cannot predict charges and is omitted in Fig. 3(d). Figure 3(e) displays the MAE of force predictions as a function of the number of training configurations. In Figs. 3(d) and 3(e), adding one message-passing layer changes the results only slightly. This is likely because for \(r \geq r_{\text {cut }}\) the LR effects dominate and are already captured by the SOG-Net layer. Regardless of whether message passing is used during the training, the CACE-SOG consistently outperforms the CACE-LES in force prediction accuracy, with MAE reductions of ~threefold-ninefold as the training set size increases. The gain in force accuracy over the CACE-only model is even more pronounced. These results suggest that the CACE-SOG may offer significant advantages over Ewald-based methods in learning LR interactions for large-scale datasets.

The superior performance of the CACE-SOG can be understood from the nature of interactions in this system. Water molecules are modeled by the SPC/Fw force field, which includes both electrostatic and Lennard-Jones (LJ) potentials for nonbonded interactions. Proper capture of \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) interactions requires an LJ cutoff of \(10-12 \AA\) for heterogeneous environments, substantially larger than the typical cutoff for the CACE and other SR descriptors

