\section*{C. Dataset with global charge transfer and different charge states}

In this section, we test the CACE-SOG model on datasets with global charge transfer or multiple charge states. The first system contains the ionic \(\mathrm{Na}_{9} \mathrm{Cl}_{8}^{+}\)and \(\mathrm{Na}_{8} \mathrm{Cl}_{8}^{+}\)clusters when a neutral Na atom is removed, \({ }^{6}\) which exhibits nontrivial charge transfer. The dataset contains 5000 configurations. We used a \(90 \%\) training and \(10 \%\) testing split. For CACE representations, we use \(r_{\text {cut }}=5.29 \AA, 6\) Bessel radial functions, \(c=8, \mu_{\text {max }}=3, v_{\text {max }}=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=2\), and no message passing ( \(T=0\) ). These choices follow prior CACE-LES settings, \({ }^{34}\) so the SR part is fixed, and we can isolate the effect of the LR layer. Adding one message-passing layer can reduce errors by enlarging the receptive field, but it also increases memory and communication costs. For the CACE-LES, we use a one-dimensional latent variable with a smearing factor of \(\sigma=1.5 \AA\). For the CACESOG, we use \(M=12\) Gaussians and a grid size of \(3 \AA\), the same as in the CACE-LES.

Table III compares the errors of the CACE-SOG, with and without the initialization strategies described in Sec. II: "w/o" (no special initialization) and "w" (both weights and bandwidths are initialized of the BSA series). We compare against the CACE-only and CACE-LES models. Note that the tested ionic clusters are dominated by electrostatics with a \(1 / r\) decay, so both the CACE-LES and CACE-SOG can be accurate. The slightly larger error arises when the CACE-SOG is trained from a naïve initialization: the initial LR decay may be mismatched, requiring more epochs, and may converge more slowly. The physics-guided initialization acts like a "normalization" prior on the LR multipliers, improving conditioning and helping prevent overfitting. One can clearly see that the CACE-SOG with the initialization achieves the lowest fitting error among all models.

