Next, we benchmark the CACE-SOG on two complex datasets: \(\mathrm{Au}_{2}-\mathrm{MgO}(001)\) and benzotriazole on \(\mathrm{Cu}(111)(\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{BTA})\), containing 5000 and 400 configurations, respectively. The data sources are Ko et al. \({ }^{6}\) and Maruf et al. \({ }^{52}\) These sets test whether an MLIP can capture charge transfer (e.g., to Au or Cu ) and interactions beyond the cutoff. We used a \(90 \%\) training and \(10 \%\) testing split. For the CACE representation, we use 6 Bessel radial functions with \(c=12, \mu_{\text {max }}=3\), \(v_{\text {max }}=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=4\), and no message-passing layer \((T=0)\). We use a cutoff radius of \(r_{\text {cut }}=5.5 \AA\) and \(r_{\text {cut }}=5 \AA\) for \(\mathrm{Au}_{2}-\mathrm{MgO}(001)\) and Cu-BTA, respectively, to match the receptive fields of other benchmark MLIPs. For the CACE-LES, we set \(k_{c}=\pi\) and \(\sigma=1 \AA\) in the Ewald summation. For CACE-SOG, we use \(M=12\) Gaussians and the same grid size. Table IV reports the energy/force RMSEs and compares the CACE-SOG with charge-constrained atomic cluster expansion (CC-ACE), \({ }^{53}\) 4G-HDNNP, \({ }^{6}\) NequIP-LR, \({ }^{52}\) the model with polarizable LR potential (Polar-LR), \({ }^{54}\) and the CACE-LES. \({ }^{34}\) When the LR tail is mainly electrostatic with \(1 / r\) decay (e.g., Cu-BTA), the CACE-SOG matches the CACE-LES and outperforms other baselines. For systems with more complex LR effects (e.g., \(\mathrm{Au}_{2}-\mathrm{MgO}(001)\) ), the CACE-SOG also outperforms the CACE-LES. Since the SOG-Net improves on an Ewald-based LR layer, other baselines are likely to benefit when paired with the SOG-Net.

