\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/896e2970-fbe3-457f-9c11-410195ba0368-09.jpg?height=817&width=1492&top_left_y=1465&top_left_x=283}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{FIG. 5. Properties of interfacial water predicted using the CACE-LES and CACE-SOG models, in comparison with the reference DFT results. (a) Snapshot of the thinner water slab configuration, (b) the oxygen density distribution, (c) the dipole density correlation function of water molecules, and (d) the average \(\cos \theta\) for the angle formed by the water dipole moment and the z-axis. Panels [(e)-(h)] show the corresponding snapshot and results for the thicker water slab.}
\end{figure}

522 water molecules. The dataset was generated using revPBE0D3 DFT and has been previously used to train LES and DeePMD models. \({ }^{13,20}\) For the CACE representation, we use a cutoff radius of \(r_{\text {cut }}=5.5 \AA\), 6 Bessel radial functions with \(c=12, \mu_{\text {max }}=3\), \(v_{\text {max }}=3, N_{\text {embedding }}=3\), and no message-passing layer ( \(T=0\) ). For the LR component, we employ a four-dimensional latent variable representation for both methods. For the CACE-LES, we set the maximum cutoff to \(k_{c}=\pi\) and the smearing factor to \(\sigma=1 \AA\) in the Ewald summation. For the CACE-SOG, we use \(M=12\) Gaussians and the same grid size as in the CACELES. To test the accuracy of predicting structural properties, we perform 500 ps molecular dynamics simulations in the NVT ensemble at 300 K using the trained CACE-LES and CACESOG models, where configurations are dumped with a period of 0.1 ps . The Nosé-Hoover thermostat \({ }^{55}\) is used with a time step of 1 fs . The CACE-LES and CACE-SOG models are trained using random splits of \(90 \%\) for training and \(10 \%\) for testing. We then simulate the liquid-vapor interface at 300 K using two different slab thicknesses: a thinner slab with a thickness of \(65 \AA\) [Fig. 5(a), which matches the slab thickness in the training DFT datasets], and a thicker slab with a thickness of \(120 \AA\) [Fig. 5(e)]. In Figs. 5(b) and 5(f), we plot the resulting density distributions for the thinner and thicker water slabs, respectively. Both the CACE-LES and CACE-SOG predict similar density profiles. These results show that the CACE-SOG model can transfer to a larger system size without retraining. For the thinner slab, both methods predict a slight boundary repulsion effect, despite the fact that the DFT dataset does not explicitly include such configurations.

In addition, we evaluate the performance of the CACE-SOG in predicting the dielectric properties and orientational order profiles of interfacial water by computing the longitudinal component (i.e., along the \(z\)-axis) of the dipole density correlation function (DDCF),
\[
\left.D_{z}\left(k_{z}\right)=\left.\langle | \rho\left(k_{z}\right)\right|^{2}\right\rangle,
\]
where \(\rho\left(k_{z}\right)\) is the Fourier transform of the molecular dipole density, and \(k_{z}\) is the \(z\)-component of the Fourier modes. We also compute the angle \(\theta\) between the dipole orientation of water molecules. The dipole correlation function at the long-wavelength limit can be used to determine the dielectric constant of water. As shown in Figs. 5(c) and 5(g), the DDCFs predicted by different MLIPs agree well for most \(k_{z}\), and \(D_{z}\left(k_{z}\right) / k_{z}^{2}\) is nearly constant at long wavelengths due to the symmetry-preserving mean field condition. \({ }^{56}\) In Figs. 5(d) and 5(h), it is shown that the two MLIPs provide slightly different predictions of orientational order, with the CACE-SOG model performing slightly better around the interface. These results demonstrate that the CACE-SOG model accurately captures the dipole layer of water molecules near the liquid-vapor interface, recovering the correct physical polarization behavior.

\section*{E. Water/Pt(111) interfacial systems}

To further assess the performance difference between the Ewald-based CACE-LES model and the SOG-based CACE-SOG model in more complex scenarios, we study a water/ \(\mathrm{Pt}(111)\) interface system. \({ }^{57}\) Water-metal interfaces are ubiquitous in nature and are relevant to a broad range of technological applications, including corrosion, electrocatalysis, environmental chemistry, and lubrication. The database consists of 48041 structures, featuring a \(3 \times 4\) orthogonal \(\operatorname{Pt}(111)\) slab with a water layer containing 32 water molecules. A snapshot is shown in Fig. 6(a). Reference energies and forces were determined using the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) functional \({ }^{58}\) combined with the D3 van der Waals correction. \({ }^{59}\) Because both electrostatics and dispersion interactions are included, the effects of the LR component are highly non-negligible. We train the CACE-LES and CACE-SOG models using the same settings as those used for the previous water datasets and then perform molecular dynamics simulations of 1 ns at \(350 K\) within the NVT ensemble, using a Nosé-Hoover thermostat with a time step of 1 fs and a characteristic damping time of 0.1 ps .

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/896e2970-fbe3-457f-9c11-410195ba0368-10.jpg?height=813&width=1125&top_left_y=1615&top_left_x=137}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{FIG. 6. Results of \(\mathrm{Pt}(111)\) interfacial water systems by the CACE-LES and CACE-SOG models, in comparison with the reference DFT results. (a) A snapshot of the system, (b) radial distribution function (RDF) of oxygen-oxygen pairs, (c) RDF of oxygen-hydrogen pairs, and (d) planar-averaged density profile of oxygen and hydrogen perpendicular to the interface.}
\end{figure}

To validate the quality of different MLIPs, we analyzed their ability to reproduce \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) and oxygen-hydrogen \((\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{H})\) RDFs. The results of this analysis are shown in Figs. 6(b) and 6(c). Evidently, there is good agreement between the RDFs obtained from our CACE-SOG model and the DFT results. However, although the CACE-LES model provides a reasonable approximation, its peak value deviates noticeably from the DFT reference and CACE-SOG results. This is likely due to the fact that the CACE-LES model is constructed using the classical Ewald summation for the \(1 / r\) correction, which cannot fully capture the joint LR effects of electrostatic and dispersion interactions.

Next, we study the characterization of the order at the interface. In Fig. 6, we display the planar-averaged density profile of water molecules as a function of distance from the Pt surface. The density of Pt-O exhibits a double-peaked structure at \(2-4 \AA\) from the Pt slab, a feature that has also been observed in previous studies of the Pt(111) interfacial water system. \({ }^{60,61}\) One can observe that the CACE-SOG matches the DFT data perfectly and correctly predicts the double-peaked structure and the peak values, demonstrating its high accuracy. This likely stems from its ability to capture LR tails, leading to a better description of interfacial adsorption forces.

\section*{IV. CONCLUDING REMARKS}

In this paper, we integrate the CACE for the SR descriptor and SOG-Net for the LR descriptor for machine learning of interatomic potentials. This results in a novel, physically interpretable CACE-SOG model that accurately captures interatomic interactions of LR systems. Unlike most previous graph-based MLIP models that heavily rely on the classical Ewald summation to account for LR interactions with a specific \(1 / r^{p}\) kernel, the use of SOG-Net enables adaptive learning of LR tails of different decay rates. We also propose an improved initialization for the Fourier multiplier that yields faster convergence and better extrapolation. Systematic experiments, including potassium fluoride aqueous solutions, molecular dimers, ionic clusters, and liquid and interfacial water systems, illustrate that the CACE-SOG model consistently achieves superior accuracy, captures LR charge transfer, predicts partial charges, and recovers structural properties. The results show almost the same accuracy as those in Ji et al., \({ }^{22}\) where the SOG-Net is used with the SR descriptor of the DP model. Notably, however, the number of trainable parameters in the CACE-SOG is reduced by more than an order of magnitude. Even when compared to the Ewald-based CACE-LES model, the CACE-SOG achieves higher accuracy while employing nearly the same number of parameters (see Table II). These results demonstrate the strong performance of the CACE-SOG and show that SOG-Net is a general module for LR interactions with variable decay tails.

In real systems, the potential energy surfaces of different systems, where LR interactions play a central role, may exhibit distinct far-field decay behaviors for each atom type. In this context, developing reliable foundation models that capture LR physics is crucial. Several challenges remain in this direction. Our future work includes extending the current model to higher-order interactions beyond two-body terms and testing it on larger datasets generated by advanced methods with quantum-mechanical accuracy. Ultimately, our goal is to learn from the data to identify the types of LR
interaction and their decay properties, providing users with a clearer physical picture.

